[{:adr/authoritative_for ["cloud-itonami-isic-5819 specialty-publishing operations-coordination actor implementation" "fresh scaffold closing the kotoba-lang/industry :spec entry gap for ISIC 5819"], :adr/topic "cloud-itonami-isic-5819-other-publishing-coverage", :adr/related ["cloud-itonami-isic-5811" "cloud-itonami-isic-5812" "ADR-2607121000" "ADR-2607152500"], :adr/status "accepted", :adr/id "adr-2617005819-cloud-itonami-isic-5819-other-publishing-coverage", :adr/doc_type "adr", :adr/last_verified "2026-07-16", :adr/body "# ADR-2617005819: cloud-itonami-isic-5819 (Other publishing activities) specialty-publishing operations-coordination actor -- fresh scaffold, full implementation\n\n**Status**: accepted\n**Date**: 2026-07-16\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki (agent-executed, standing authorization)\n**Related**: ADR-2607121000 (Wave 3, production/robotics), ADR-2607152500\n(Wave 4, human-facing/personal services -- authorizes this batch to\nproceed in parallel with Wave 3), cloud-itonami-isic-5811 (Book\npublishing -- the closest-domain reference module shape this actor\nmirrors op-for-op, independently re-read in full before use),\ncloud-itonami-isic-5812 (Publishing of directories and mailing lists --\nalso independently re-read in full), the `kotoba-lang/industry`\nregistry's `\"5819\"` catalog entry\n\n## Context\n\n`kotoba-lang/industry`'s registry carried a `\"5819\"` entry at\n`:maturity :spec` with `:name \"Other publishing activities\"` (matching\nthe assigned ISIC class, independently verified via a fresh clone\nbefore any work began) and `:repo\n\"https://github.com/gftdcojp/cloud-itonami-J5819\"` (an old,\nnever-populated placeholder naming scheme). Confirmed no repo exists yet\nat either that stale placeholder or the real\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-5819` target (`gh api` 404 for both)\nbefore any work began.\n\nISIC 5819 is the residual \"other publishing activities\" class: catalogs,\ngreeting cards, calendars, posters, advertising material, forms, and\nother printed matter not elsewhere classified (i.e. not books [5811],\nnewspapers [5813], directories/mailing lists [5812], or software\n[5820]). This is part of Wave 4 (human-facing/personal-services fleet,\nISIC sections I/P/Q/R/S/T), running in parallel with the ongoing Wave 3\n(production/robotics) rollout per ADR-2607152500. Like book publishing,\n\"other publishing\" touches editorial-content-decision authority and\nlegal-risk-clearance decisions (copyright/trademark/licensed-character\nclearance for a card/calendar/poster design), so this actor is\ndeliberately scoped to back-office operations coordination only, with a\nclosed op allowlist that structurally excludes finalizing an\neditorial-content decision or a legal-risk clearance decision -- those\nremain exclusively human/institutional decisions, never an\nauto-commit-eligible op, per Wave 4's person-facing-service safety\nguardrail.\n\n## Decision\n\nScaffold `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-5819` as a specialty-\npublishing OPERATIONS COORDINATION actor (not editorial-content-decision\nauthority, not a legal-risk-clearance authority), with a concrete\nillustrative product line -- a specialty publisher producing greeting\ncards, calendars, and posters -- documented plainly in the README.\nMirrors `cloud-itonami-isic-5811`'s verified module shape and op-set\n(`store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`phase`/`operation`/`sim`,\n`deps.edn`/`blueprint.edn`/README/GOVERNANCE/CODE_OF_CONDUCT/\nCONTRIBUTING/SECURITY, AGPL-3.0-or-later) op-for-op, since the domain\ndesign specified for this class uses the identical four op names as\nbook publishing, with fresh, specialty-publishing-specific domain logic\nunder the `specpubops` namespace:\n\n1. **`specpubops.store`** -- `MemStore` (atom of EDN) behind a `Store`\n   protocol; a `titles` directory keyed by `:title-id` STRING (a card/\n   calendar/poster/catalog title in production), plus an append-only\n   `ledger` and a `coordination-log` of committed records.\n2. **`specpubops.advisor`** (\"SpecPubAdvisor\") -- deterministic mock\n   advisor drafting exactly four kinds of proposal: production-record\n   logging (design-status/print-run-quantity/finish-spec data),\n   production-operation scheduling (design/proofing/print-run\n   scheduling), distribution coordination (outbound retail/wholesale\n   shipment), and content-concern flagging (copyright/trademark/\n   licensed-character similarity, cultural/seasonal sensitivity, or\n   suspected plagiarism of another publisher's design). Every\n   proposal's `:effect` is always `:propose`; every output is censored\n   downstream by the governor.\n3. **`specpubops.governor`** (\"SpecPubGovernor\") -- three HARD checks,\n   all permanent and un-overridable: (1) title-unverified -- the\n   target title record must exist AND be independently\n   `:registered?`/`:verified?` in the store before ANY proposal for it\n   may commit or escalate; (2) effect-not-propose -- any `:effect`\n   other than `:propose` is HARD-blocked; (3) scope-exclusion -- any\n   proposal (regardless of op) whose op/summary/rationale/cites/value\n   touches finalizing-an-editorial-content-decision or\n   legal-risk-clearance territory is a HARD, PERMANENT block,\n   unconditionally evaluated on every proposal; an op outside the\n   closed four-op allowlist is folded into this same check. One\n   ESCALATE (soft) gate: `:flag-content-concern` ALWAYS escalates to a\n   human regardless of confidence, as does low confidence generally.\n4. **`specpubops.phase`** -- Phase 0->3 staged rollout;\n   `:flag-content-concern` is permanently ABSENT from every phase's\n   `:auto` set (structural fact, not a rollout milestone still to\n   come) -- only `:log-production-record`/`:schedule-production-\n   operation`/`:coordinate-distribution` may auto-commit at phase 3\n   when governor-clean.\n5. **`specpubops.operation`** (\"OperationActor\") -- langgraph-clj\n   StateGraph, `intake -> advise -> govern -> decide -> commit | hold |\n   request-approval`, `interrupt-before #{:request-approval}` for\n   human-in-the-loop sign-off, invoked exclusively via\n   `langgraph.graph/run*`.\n6. **`deps.edn` / `blueprint.edn` / docs** -- mirror\n   `cloud-itonami-isic-5811`'s shape (`:test`/`:lint`/`:run`/`:dev`\n   aliases pinned to the same `kotoba-lang/langgraph` git SHA\n   `a332a770a0d2b5193f81b54483bb954fb29ef8d7`, `itonami.blueprint/*`\n   metadata, scope/design/testing README sections).\n\n### Known self-tripping bug class -- guarded against from the start\n\nThis fleet has independently rediscovered, in several sibling ISIC\nactors, the same bug class: a scope-exclusion term phrased as a bare\nnoun (e.g. \"copyright\") accidentally matches inside the mock advisor's\nown default rationale/disclaimer text for a legitimate, allowed\nproposal, causing the actor to self-block on its own happy path. Every\nterm in `specpubops.governor/scope-excluded-terms` is phrased as a\nfinalization/execution ACTION (\"legal-risk clearance\", \"finalize the\neditorial-content decision\"), never a bare noun, and\n`test/specpubops/advisor_test.clj` carries a dedicated regression test\n(`default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion`)\nasserting every op's own default proposal text clears the governor's\nscope-exclusion scan.\n\n### What this actor does NOT do\n\nFinalizing an editorial-content decision (what a card/calendar/poster\ndesign actually depicts, whether it ships as designed), and issuing a\nlegal-risk clearance decision (copyright/trademark/licensed-character\nclearance), both remain exclusively human/institutional decisions,\npermanently, with no actor or human-approval override path -- enforced\nstructurally by the governor's closed op/effect allowlists and the\nunconditional scope-exclusion check, not just documented.\n`:flag-content-concern` is a \"surface the concern\" op only; it can\nnever self-clear the concern it raises, and is never a member of any\nphase's `:auto` set.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `cloud-itonami-isic-5819`: `clojure -M:dev:test` -- raw final line:\n  `Ran 46 tests containing 129 assertions.` / `0 failures, 0 errors.`\n  (re-run green a second time from a brand-new fresh clone after push,\n  with fresh `kotoba-lang/langgraph`/`kotoba-lang/langchain` siblings).\n- `clojure -M:lint` -- 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- `clojure -M:dev:run` demo narrative exercises proposal submission,\n  escalation/approval on every write op, and every HARD-hold scenario\n  directly (unregistered title, unverified title, non-`:propose`\n  effect, editorial-content/legal-clearance scope drift) -- ran clean,\n  no exceptions.\n- All source under `src/`/`test/` is `.cljc`, no JVM-only interop; the\n  actor graph is invoked exclusively via `langgraph.graph/run*`.\n- Repo created fresh (`gh repo create` + push, public visibility\n  matching the reference and `blueprint.edn`'s `:status :public-oss`),\n  initial commit `8fbedcc` on `cloud-itonami-isic-5819`'s `main` (no\n  prior history), confirmed via a brand-new fresh clone.\n- `kotoba-lang/industry` registry `\"5819\"` entry updated in place\n  (exact-text edit of the literal `{:id \"5819\" ...}` block only, no\n  other entry's block touched, byte-offset-diff-verified confined to\n  the intended `:repo`/`:business-id`/`:maturity` fields):\n  `:repo`/`:business-id` corrected from the never-populated\n  `gftdcojp/cloud-itonami-J5819` naming to\n  `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-5819` / `cloud-itonami-isic-5819`,\n  `:maturity` `:spec` -> `:implemented`; `:name` was already the full,\n  correct \"Other publishing activities\" (not affected by the\n  ~10%-of-entries truncated-name seed-data bug, so no de-truncation was\n  needed); `:required-technologies`/`:optional-technologies`/\n  `:operating-states` left as the existing placeholder shape, out of\n  this promotion's minimal-diff scope. Landed via a Contents API\n  single-file PUT (sha-checked optimistic concurrency, immediately\n  re-fetched fresh content before the PUT per this fleet's\n  hot-contention discipline), landed on the first attempt with no 409\n  retries needed, commit `879caa4ea46e56435413476fdf6b7d2afb4894a8`.\n- `test/kotoba/industry_test.clj`'s `maturity-summary` assertion\n  bumped with a dedicated testing block for `\"5819\"`, live-recomputed\n  via `(kotoba.industry/maturity-summary)` against freshly re-fetched\n  content immediately before each PUT attempt (never a reused/stale\n  buffer) -- this file is an extremely hot, high-concurrency shared\n  file across the fleet at the time of this edit: the first PUT attempt\n  (built against a live count of 381) 409'd on stale sha; a rebuilt\n  second attempt (count 383, after further concurrent registry\n  promotions) also 409'd on stale sha before a third attempt (still\n  count 383, sha re-checked immediately beforehand) landed, commit\n  `cacd0fcba1ec44b48036872667bc60be71ea7360`. A brand-new fresh-clone\n  re-run of the full `kotoba-lang/industry` suite immediately afterward\n  caught the assertion already stale again (`383` vs a live `384`, from\n  a concurrent sibling agent's own registry promotion landing in the\n  interim) -- a corrective PUT was queued but a concurrent sibling\n  agent's own independent fix (`385`, one further promotion beyond the\n  `384` this agent had observed) landed first, confirmed matching a\n  fresh `(kotoba.industry/maturity-summary)` recompute immediately\n  after. Documented here in full per this fleet's honest-reporting\n  norm, matching the transparency-note precedent set by\n  ADR-2616581100 (cloud-itonami-isic-5811).\n- Final post-merge re-verification (brand-new scratch directory, fresh\n  `origin/main` clone of both `cloud-itonami-isic-5819` and\n  `kotoba-lang/industry`, plus fresh `kotoba-lang/technology`/\n  `kotoba-lang/langgraph`/`kotoba-lang/langchain` siblings):\n  `clojure -M:dev:test` on `cloud-itonami-isic-5819` re-run green\n  (`Ran 46 tests containing 129 assertions.` / `0 failures, 0 errors.`);\n  `clojure -M:test` on `kotoba-lang/industry` re-run green\n  (`Ran 15 tests containing 1048 assertions.` / `0 failures, 0 errors.`);\n  the registry's `\"5819\"` entry confirmed `:maturity :implemented` with\n  the corrected `:repo`/`:business-id`, and a sample of sibling entries\n  (`5811`/`5812`/`8730`) confirmed untouched. `grep` for the UTF-8\n  replacement character against `resources/kotoba/industry/registry.edn`\n  returned `0` (no file-wide mojibake).\n\n## Consequences\n\n(+) `cloud-itonami-isic-5819` now has a real, tested implementation\nmatching the shape of every other cloud-itonami ISIC actor, replacing a\n`:spec` placeholder pointing at a repo that never existed.\n\n(+) `kotoba-lang/industry` registry `\"5819\"` entry promoted to\n`:maturity :implemented`.\n\n(+) This actor is another concrete example of Wave 4's\nperson-facing-service safety guardrail applied to the publishing\nsector's editorial-content/legal-risk-clearance decision axis, using\nthe same closed-allowlist + unconditional-scope-exclusion pattern as\n`cloud-itonami-isic-5811` and `cloud-itonami-isic-5812`, adapted to the\nISIC 5819 residual \"other publishing\" category via a concrete greeting-\ncard/calendar/poster illustrative product line.\n\n(-) Still a simulation/proposal layer, not a real publishing-operations\nsystem. Editorial decisions and legal-risk clearance remain\nhuman-/institution-controlled via external channels.\n\n(-) No integration with real publishing-house systems (design/proof\nmanagement, print-run scheduling, licensing/rights databases,\ndistribution partner APIs) -- this is a standalone coordinator\nblueprint, matching every prior sibling actor's own stated limitation.", :adr/deciders ["Jun Kawasaki"], :adr/date "2026-07-16", :db/id -1, :adr/authoritative true, :adr/title "ADR-2617005819: cloud-itonami-isic-5819 (Other publishing activities) specialty-publishing operations-coordination actor -- fresh scaffold, full implementation", :adr/source-format "edn"}]